+# FICTION. Episode 3: Which basis?
+
+**Saturday 24 October 2026, Sydney · Point of view: Jun · TNA closes at 08:00 today; Keel submitted last night. Vote tomorrow.**
+
+---
+
+Jun posted at 6:58, as always, with his coffee. Then he deleted it and posted again at 7:04.
+
+**Jun:** Correction. Don't quote $293,928 again. It was true on Wednesday.
+
+The Treasury had put up Thursday's close overnight. Yields had fallen about 0.17 points in a day: the 10-year from 5.13
+to 4.96, the 20-year from 5.47 to 5.32. He had run the ladder twice, the way he'd promised the team he would since
+Tess wrote *never quote a ladder cost without its basis* across the top of his sheet.
+
+**Jun:** Payment-date basis: $298,861. Room under $300k: $1,139. Holdings basis, each $50k counted from the day the
+bond we'd actually hold matures: $302,199. That's $2,199 *over*. MODEL, 22 Oct close.
+
+**Kai:** Four basis points of room on the nice basis and minus seven and a half on the real one. My phone battery has more
+margin than Laura's first payment.
+
+**Jun:** One day cost about $4,900. And it's the Feb-2037 bond doing most of the damage. Its money sits ten and a half
+months before the 2038 payment. That gap alone is $1,406.
+
+**Nora:** So what?
+
+**Jun:** So the thing we said might happen in January has already happened on paper. If January looks like Thursday,
+the 2028 deposit tops up the ladder before anything else.
+
+He'd checked it three ways before breakfast. Two methods agreed to within eight dollars. A smoother curve fit came
+out about $500 lower and still over the line. *Same sign*, he wrote in his notebook, and underlined it.
+
+---
+
+### Library, 11 a.m.
+
+Nora had the whiteboard photo on the table: *Sun 25 Oct, vote: sail, reserve rule, certainty.* Tess spoke first, as
+agreed. She had the IPS draft printed, two lines circled in red.
+
+**Tess:** Principle one says the gap is "the first use of the 2028 deposit". Same paragraph: every payment is "paid in
+full from bonds Laura already owns". Read them together.
+
+**Kai:** That's my whole speech. I'm not fighting the order. Latest payments first is right; the gap sits in the short
+2033 bond, not the fourteen-year one. I'm fighting the sentence. For one year, she doesn't own all of them. The 2033
+payment is an IOU against her 2028 earnings, and we don't say so.
+
+**Felix:** Can I read you something? Client case, line 43: she "will contribute" the $150,000. Lines 63 and 64 call it one of
+"the two contributions". Line 92 bans co-sponsors and grants, not her own deposit. So leaning on it is allowed.
+
+**Kai:** Allowed isn't the same as honest.
+
+**Felix:** No. Line 146: teams should "identify and explain their assumptions about ... the timing of cash flows". A
+rung that waits a year *is* a cash-flow timing assumption. It has to be in the IPS in words, because Investment Policy
+line 74 says we can't revise the strategy after the deadline.
+
+**Amara:** Then tell me which part shrinks. The draft never says. Jun's table says the sail pays. Is that true?
+
+**Jun:** If we write it that way. Order of the 2028 money: finish the ladder, buy the floor, then the sail. Then the
+floor is a fixed size and the sail takes the hit. A $2,199 gap costs about $2,900 of sail by 2033.
+
+**Amara:** Good. Because what scares me is a co-sponsor hearing a minimum in 2031 that something can still take from.
+Line 114: if she promises more than she can give, she "could damage her credibility". By 2031 the floor should be a
+bond she holds, not a forecast.
+
+**Nora:** And the IPS gets the dollar figure?
+
+**Felix:** Investment Policy, lines 68 to 69. Not expected: "final operating-reserve calculations ... a final
+facility-contribution range". The rule goes in now. The dollars go in December.
+
+**Jun:** Good, because any number we write today is stale by January. It moved $4,900 overnight.
+
+Kai leaned back.
+
+**Kai:** Fine. Keep the order, change the words. Say the gap out loud, give the 2028 money a spending order, and our
+honesty rule is true again. *That's* the bold bit. Not more stocks.
+
+**Nora:** Write it down before he changes his mind.
+
+---
+
+Felix waited until the end. He always did.
+
+**Felix:** One more. The FAQ changed. I read it this morning. Under "NOT permitted" it now says "*Individual* TIPS" and
+"*Individual* STRIPS". The Week 4 email says plain "TIPS".
+
+**Amara:** So a TIPS fund might be allowed?
+
+**Felix:** Might. The two sources disagree, and we can't ask Wharton ourselves. Until someone asks, we treat it the
+way the email reads. Nothing in our plan changes.
+
+Amara wrote *inflation: still a Final Report assumption* next to her parked line, and didn't argue.
+
+Nora read the board out at 12:14.
+
+---
